et me check whether there's some other mechanism. Perhaps dark-factory sets ROOTDIR in the environment, or the `.dark-factory.yaml` has something. Let me look.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe git actually works despite `.git` being a char device? Let me test `git rev-parse`. The earlier `make test` output showed `fatal: not a git repository (or any parent up to mount point /)` — so git fails. That's why ROOTDIR is empty.\n\nSo to run `make precommit` in this container, I need ROOTDIR to resolve. Options:\n- `make ROOTDIR=/workspace precommit` (pass ROOTDIR as a make var — since ROOTDIR uses `?=`, an environment variable would be overridden... wait `?=` means \"set if not already set\". If I pass ROOTDIR on the command line as a make argument, it overrides. If I export ROOTDIR=/workspace in the environment, `?=` respects it since it's already set. Actually with `?=`, if the variable is set in the environment, make keeps the environment value. Yes.)\n- Set GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false to avoid git VCS stamping during go build.\n\nLet me verify:\n1. `git rev-parse` fails (confirmed by earlier output).\n2. `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false go build ./...` works.\n3. `make ROOTDIR=/workspace test` works.\n\nBut wait — there's a bigger concern. The system reminder explicitly says: \"When hideGit=true active, GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false is typically already set in the container environment, so go test, errcheck, gosec, golangci-lint, and other static analysis tools work normally without git version control metadata. You do not need to work around this.\"\n\nBut in THIS container, GOFLAGS is NOT set. So I DO need to work around the buildvcs issue. The reminder says \"You do not need to work around this\" — but that's predicated on GOFLAGS being set, which it isn't here. Hmm. But the reminder also says \"Regardless of how /workspace/.git appears ... run the project's precommit gate (make precommit or the equivalent validation command) to validate your changes before reporting completion